Step 1: Customer Entity
public class Customer {

    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;

    public Customer(Long id, String name, String email) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public String getEmail() {
        return email;
    }
}
Step 2: Repository

Normally in Spring Boot:

Optional<Customer> findById(Long id);

Suppose it returns

Optional<Customer> customer =
Optional.of(new Customer(101L,"Akhila","akhila@gmail.com"));

Now let's see all the Optional methods.

1. isPresent()
if(customer.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println("Customer Found");
}

Output

Customer Found
Interview Answer

Checks whether Optional contains a value.

2. ifPresent()

Instead of writing

if(customer.isPresent()){
    System.out.println(customer.get().getName());
}

Write

customer.ifPresent(c ->
        System.out.println(c.getName()));

Output

Akhila
Interview Answer

Executes the lambda only if the value exists.

3. get()
Customer c = customer.get();

System.out.println(c.getEmail());

Output

akhila@gmail.com

⚠️ Never use

Optional.empty().get();

It throws

NoSuchElementException
4. orElse()

Suppose customer doesn't exist.

Optional<Customer> customer =
Optional.empty();

Customer c = customer.orElse(
        new Customer(0L,"Guest","guest@gmail.com"));

System.out.println(c.getName());

Output

Guest
Interview Answer

Returns the value if present; otherwise returns the default object.

5. orElseGet()
Customer c = customer.orElseGet(() ->
        new Customer(0L,"Generated User","guest@gmail.com"));

Difference:
orElse()
Always creates the object.

orElseGet()

Creates it only if needed.

6. orElseThrow()

This is the most common one in Spring Boot.

Customer c = repository.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow(() ->
        new RuntimeException("Customer Not Found"));

If customer doesn't exist

Output

RuntimeException:
Customer Not Found

This is exactly how we use it in projects.

7. map()

Suppose you only need the customer's email.

Without map

Customer c = customer.get();

System.out.println(c.getEmail());

Using map

Optional<String> email =
customer.map(Customer::getEmail);

System.out.println(email.get());

Output

akhila@gmail.com
Interview Answer

map() transforms the object inside the Optional into another value.

8. filter()

Suppose only Gmail users are allowed.

customer.filter(c ->
        c.getEmail().endsWith("@gmail.com"))
        .ifPresent(System.out::println);

If email is Gmail

Output

Customer object

If not Gmail

Nothing is printed.

9. of()
Optional<String> name =
Optional.of("Akhila");

Never do

Optional.of(null);

Throws

NullPointerException
10. ofNullable()
String email = null;

Optional<String> mail =
Optional.ofNullable(email);

Output

Optional.empty

No exception.

Complete Real-Time Example
Optional<Customer> customer =
repository.findById(101L);

//1
System.out.println(customer.isPresent());

//2
customer.ifPresent(c ->
System.out.println(c.getName()));

//3
Customer c1 =
customer.orElse(
new Customer(0L,"Guest","guest@gmail.com"));

//4
Customer c2 =
customer.orElseGet(()->
new Customer(0L,"Generated User","generated@gmail.com"));

//5
Customer c3 =
customer.orElseThrow(()->
new RuntimeException("Customer Not Found"));

//6
Optional<String> email =
customer.map(Customer::getEmail);

//7
customer.filter(c->
c.getEmail().endsWith("@gmail.com"))
.ifPresent(System.out::println);
How It Works (Flow)
Repository
      │
      ▼
Optional<Customer>
      │
      ├── isPresent() → true/false
      │
      ├── ifPresent() → Execute lambda
      │
      ├── get() → Return object
      │
      ├── orElse() → Default object
      │
      ├── orElseGet() → Lazily create object
      │
      ├── orElseThrow() → Throw exception
      │
      ├── map() → Convert Customer → Email
      │
      └── filter() → Apply condition
Easy Trick to Remember
Method	Purpose
of()	Create Optional (null not allowed)
ofNullable()	Create Optional (null allowed)
isPresent()	Check if value exists
ifPresent()	Execute code if value exists
get()	Get the value (avoid if possible)
orElse()	Return default value
orElseGet()	Lazily create default value
orElseThrow()	Throw exception if empty
map()	Transform the value
filter()	Apply a condition
💡 Interview Tip (Very Important)

If an interviewer asks:

"Have you used Optional in your project?"

You can confidently say:

"Yes. In our Spring Boot application, Spring Data JPA repository methods like findById() return Optional<Customer>.
In the service layer, we usually use orElseThrow() to throw a custom CustomerNotFoundException if the customer is not found.
Sometimes we use map() when we only need a specific field from the entity, and ifPresent() when an operation should execute only
if the value exists."
